  "review_text": "[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=extralarge; text=<b>1963 End of the Year Review</b>]\nNew to the awards?&nbsp; See the [PAGE: awards-index] for full descriptions.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n1963 brought us <b>78</b> new books -- not counting [PAGE: meanwhile] -- containing <b>88</b> actually reviewed and graded stories.\n\nThe output nearly doubled in terms of actually-published issues from 1962 to 1963 (38 to 78) and <i>more</i> than doubled in terms of stories (41 to 88), though the total <i>weight</i> was only just over 59 (meaning just over 59 full-sized books times 20 pages worth of material -- it means that there were a <i>lot</i> of partial books on the shelves this year).\n\nIn the end, the final weighted average was [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=4.48], which included 23 stories that were at least mid-grade (6.0 or better), though 45 (nearly double) were <i>below</i> readable (less than 4.0), so the net result was that, while stories <i>could</i> occasionally be good, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=readers were forced to sift through a lot of baggage to find them.]\n\nFurthermore, there were precious few serviceable, middle-of-the-road stories to be found, meaning that the normal experience tended to be stories that were either [SPAN: severity=success; text=solid (at times, even excellent)]... or [SPAN: severity=error; text=poor (at times, even abysmal)].&nbsp; And sadly, the bad more than outweighed the good in any given month.\n\nThe uneven reading experience undoubtedly left buyers still clinging to the promise of something great in the works, without the consistency needed to convince them that Marvel held any long-term alternative to more... <i>uh</i>... [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=Dominant Competitors].\n\nSpider-Man was already proving what Marvel <i>could</i> be, but much of the rest of the line was still struggling to reach that level consistently (see the <i>State of the Line Report</i> below for more detail).\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Introductions</b>]\n\nMajor introductions included:\n[HANG] * Doctor Strange\n[HANG] * Wasp\n[HANG] * Avengers\n[HANG] * X-Men\n[HANG] * (Giant-Man also debuts, though he's really just Ant-Man under a new label.)\n\nThe year also introduced other characters such as the Watcher, Vulture, Mad Thinker, Doctor Octopus, Sandman, Baron Mordo, Betty Brant, Super-Skrull, Pepper Potts, Happy Hogan, Lady Dorma, Warlord Krang, Porcupine, Magneto, Rama Tut, Molecule Man, Crimson Dynamo, Lizard, Melter, Cobra, Mister Hyde, Vanisher, the Blob, Electro, Mandarin, the Enforcers, to name a few.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Top Moments of the Year</b>]\n[HANG] * The Captain America try-out in <i>Strange Tales </i>#114 leads to the decision to bring back the character for good.\n[HANG] * The Hulk quitting the Avengers in only the second issue likely comes as a complete surprise to all readers.\n[HANG] * Iron Man's appearance changes a couple of times in 1963, finally settling closer (but not quite getting) to the longtime look he eventually gets.\n[HANG] * Peter Parker decks Flash Thompson in the boxing ring.\n[HANG] * Sue Storm's new invisible force field power is a welcome addition and finally makes her equally as formidable as the other three have always been.\n[HANG] * Ben's outcry of \"<i>It's Clobberin' Time!</i>\" and mention of his Aunt Petunia add depth to his already interesting personality.\n\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Other Notable Observations about the Year</b>]\n[HANG] * It seems that Dr. Doom and the Sub-Mariner were lurking around every corner.\n[HANG] * Even though I'm not reviewing <i>Sgt. Fury</i>, Nick Fury is staged to play a role in the Marvel Universe (and appears this year in [LINK:1963:FF_21|FF #21|2027-01-23]).\n[HANG] * The <i>Incredible Hulk</i> was canceled (which came as no surprise, as it was consistently dreadful).\n[HANG] * <i>Strange Tales</i> becomes the first book to feature co-stars, with Human Torch and Doctor Strange sharing the spotlight.\n[HANG] * The <i>Tales of Asgard</i> feature in <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> debuts and seems to be a solid success.\n[HANG] * <i>Tales of the Watcher</i> back-up in TOS reads better than <i>The Wasp Tells a Tale</i> in TTA.&nbsp; The Watcher's role is put to far better use.\n[HANG] * Spider-Man's supporting cast is clearly the most developed of all the mags, <i>far</i> superior to the supporting casts around it, namely Jane Foster, Alicia Masters, Rick Jones, Pepper Potts and Happy Hogan (and just who else is there besides these anyway?).\n[BR]\n[BR] While the last issue of X-Men in 1963 hinted that their personalities might be worth paying attention to, they're the <i>main</i> characters and not the <i>supporting</i> ones.&nbsp; The wealth that's available around Peter Parker is immense, and issues 8 through 10 in particular drive home how his full cast can play a nice role in the crafting of good stories.\n\n[HANG] * Doctor Strange has done nothing yet to make a reader look forward to his monthly feature, which remains heavy on atmosphere and light on payoff (and... <i>well</i>... neither has Ant-Man/Giant-Man).\n[HANG] * Marvel has multiple crossover stories involving Spider-Man and the Human Torch, the FF teamed up with both Ant-Man and Nick Fury, and Iron Man tangled with the Angel.&nbsp; The shared Marvel Universe is <i>real</i>.\n[HANG] * The early issues of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> really <i>do</i> stand out in terms of quality, so much so that the magazine quickly shifted to a monthly release schedule.\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=success; display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Best Story of the Year</b>]\n\nSince the <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> absolutely <b>dominated</b> the top five spots in terms of quality, with issues 2 (first story), 3, 4, 9 and 10 (with only [LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25] even joining it with a grade at or above an 8.0 for the entire year), there was little doubt one of those issues would take home this year's trophy.\n\nSo since it was the best-crafted story of the year, [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Amazing Spider-Man 10] gets the nod ([LINK:1963:ASM_10|ASM #10|2027-04-05]).\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=error; display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Trash Heap of the Year</b>]\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6] -- the ill-fated Metal Master showed us that no matter how bad the <i>previous </i>issues of the Hulk magazine had been, [SPAN: severity=error; text=we hadn't seen <i>anything</i> yet!]\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14] -- no matter what problem the Sub-Mariner faced, he had a sea creature at his disposal perfectly suited to deal with it (in what felt like it should be a drinking game)\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08] -- the Space Phantom debuts in a tale riddled with so many plot holes, it would make any reader's head spin -- <i>yeesh</i>!\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:ST_117_torch1|ST #117torch|2027-03-19] -- While neither of the two stories got rock-bottom marks, it was the brutal <i>pair</i> of 1.8 ratings that left readers with a bad taste in their mouths.\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:FF_24|FF #24|2027-04-08] -- The Infant Terrible?&nbsp; I don't think so!&nbsp; <i>Issue Terrible</i> was more like it!\n\nAll were horrible in their own way, and it's quite astonishing that [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6] is there, especially since it was his issue 5 that won <i>last year's</i> award (and no doubt ultimately led to the demise of the book).&nbsp; I'll go easy on the <i>Hulk</i> title since it only appeared for a single month and instead target the second issue of Avengers (whose worst moments <i>also</i> featured the Hulk -- of course)!\n\nThus, it is [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Avengers 2] that takes the... <i>uh</i>... \"bottom\" spot this year!\n\nCover your heads, Stan and Jack!&nbsp; You've managed this dubious honor two years in a row!\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=error; display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Shaft Award</b>]\n\nOne story deserves a special callout for its blatantly offensive nature, which is the Iron Man story featuring the <i>first</i> Dr. Strange!&nbsp; <i>Tales of Suspense</i> 41 ([LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41]) was certainly such an issue.&nbsp; (And I note that it could have easily also been a finalist for the <b>Spiral Award</b> as well since we go from villain introduction to cheers from the \"power-mad military men\" assembled from all over the world -- in just three story pages!)\n\nBecause it was a <i>flashlight</i> that ultimately saved the title hero from this would-be dictator, I feel it is necessary to... <i>err</i>... \"shine a light\" on this crime against readership!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Holy cow!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Top Covers</b>]\n\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3] -- the shadowy form of Doctor Octopus holds Spider-Man in his grip!\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:AVEN_1|AVEN #1|2026-11-13] -- mainly because of reader excitement at seeing all those heroes together in one magazine!\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:ST_115_torch1|ST #115torch|2027-01-26] -- the Human Torch against the Sandman?&nbsp; \"Spider-Man's Fatal Foe!\"\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:ASM_8|ASM #8|2027-02-12] -- the content was solid (at least in the first of the two stories), but the cover was memorable\n\nNone of these rise to levels of greatness normally associated with \"greatest covers of all time,\" but <i>certainly</i> the creepy and obviously powerful Doctor Octopus was probably the most impressive.\n\nSo I award the honor to [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Amazing Spider-Man 3].\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Best Panel of the Year</b>]\n\n[ASSET: left | ThingHasHadEnough | width=12%] [ASSET: right | DocOckFrontLit | width=12%] [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14] -- bottom of page 16 -- the Thing has had <i>enough</i> of the Sub-Mariner's deep sea fungus (and apparently of Namor's endless chatter)!\n[BR]\n[BR] [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3] -- front lit Doctor Octopus!\n\n[ASSET: left | SpiderManBurstsIn | width=16%] [LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26] -- Spider-Man bursts in on the Sandman!\n\n[ASSET: left | HammerStrike | width=10%] [ASSET: right | FlashBrained | width=12%] [LINK:1963:JIM_98_thor0|JIM #98|2027-01-11] -- Thor revels in the thrill of battle!\n[BR]\n[BR] [LINK:1963:ASM_8|ASM #8|2027-02-12] -- Flash Thompson gets brained by \"puny\" Peter Parker in the boxing ring\n\nAll were worthy candidates, and the sight of seeing Thor in full-on Asgardian battle mode certainly elicited a thrill, but so did Peter Parker's well-deserved blow to his nemesis Flash Thompson.\n\nDon Heck's Thor panel thus falls to second place behind Steve Ditko's <i>perfectly drawn</i> near-knock-out of Flash in the boxing ring!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Even <i>Peter</i> was stunned -- as were we <b>all</b>!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Great job, Steve!]\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Best Back-Up Story</b>]\n\nThere were actually some pretty good back-up tales to be found buried amidst this year's batch of books.\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_44_iron0|TOS #44] -- \"One of Us Must Die\"\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_47_iron0|TOS #47|2027-01-02] -- \"Shock!\"\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09] -- \"No Place to Turn!\"\n\nI laughed at the end of <i>Shock</i> and thought <i>One of Us Must Die </i>was an actually solid read (albeit quite <i>short</i>).&nbsp; But none of them held a candle to [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23]'s \"Target... Earth!\"\n\nSince that one was primarily memorable for its one laugh-out-loud moment, I'll save it for Best Belly-Laugh Moment below.&nbsp; It was funny enough that I'll <i>never</i> forget that final panel!\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Best Belly-Laugh Moment</b>]\n\nThere were some genuinely funny moments this year:\n\n[ASSET: left | JamesonVulture | width=16%] [ASSET: right | SamsDelicatessen | width=11%] [LINK:1963:ASM_7|ASM #7|2027-01-20] -- The sight of J. Jonah Jameson arguing with the Vulture, trying to convince him <i>not</i> to rob his payroll, <i>heh</i>.\n[BR]\n[BR] [LINK:1963:FF_22|FF #22|2027-02-17] -- Reed's look of exasperation on page 7, panel 3, having to explain to a \"Mrs. Frobush\" on the phone that she has the wrong number, that \"This <i>isn't</i> Sam's Delicatessen!\"\n\n[ASSET: left | SlideRule | width=12%] [LINK:1963:TOS_50_iron0|TOS #50|2027-03-24] -- Iron Man unveils his \"built-in slide-rule\" in a laugh-out-loud moment that \"amuses\" even the Mandarin, just as he's about to strike!\n\n[DEVNOTE: Target...Earth!&nbsp; (put here for searching purposes)]\n\nBut for me, the funniest moment of 1963 was the laugh-out-loud (almost spewing coffee onto my monitor) moment at the end of the <b>backup</b> tale from [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23], which was called \"Target...Earth!\" (by Stan Lee and Steve Ditko).\n\nVery much in line with one of 1961's Trope Trifecta, this one detailed Ba-Korr and the warriors of the planet Klang, who are looking for a new world to conquer, and lo and behold they find \"a weak planet named Earth!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(<i>Oh no!</i>)]&nbsp; \"in a far-off galaxy\" no less!&nbsp; Ba-Korr sends Sagar, his mightiest warrior to scout Earth's defenses... and get <i>this</i> -- if he doesn't report back within 48 hours, the invasion will be called off!\n\nAnd so, with only 48 hours to do his scouting job, Sagar is somehow able to travel to the \"far-off galaxy\" and find that Earth will prove no match for their forces, [SPAN: severity=info; text=but he never reports back, so the invasion is called off.]&nbsp; Why, you ask?&nbsp; Because of where he parked his ship.&nbsp; When he goes back to his ship to signal to Ba-Korr that the invasion should proceed as planned, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he finds his ship gone -- he's trapped!]\n\nThe story's final panel that reveals why the ship disappeared became the year's best laugh-out-loud moment.\n[ASSET: center | SlowSinkingQuicksand | width=67%]\n\nHe parked his spaceship in a bog of \"slow-sinking quicksand.\"&nbsp; And to add to the humor, he concludes that, \"There is but one thing to do... I must <i>follow</i> my ship to the bottom of this bog -- to remain there for all eternity -- to pay for my fatal error!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>OMG!</i>]<i>  </i>What a silly story, but one with such an ironic ending that I burst out laughing!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>Hahahaha!</i>]\n\nOnce again, these aliens from \"far-off\" galaxies are somehow able to detect Earth and want to conquer it, even though there must be countless planets in their <i>own</i> galaxies that would be much more convenient!&nbsp; But, perhaps not!&nbsp; I mean, if you're able to travel to Earth <i>and</i> back within a mere 48 hours, despite the distance, perhaps <i>any</i> planet is convenient!\n\n[ASSET: left | CreepyTinkerer | width=13%] However, I also want to give out an Honorable Mention for the face of the Tinkerer in the second story that made up [LINK:1963:ASM_2_asm1|ASM #2].&nbsp; Steve Ditko strikes just the right look for a character who turns out to be every bit as creepy as this panel shows him to be.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Not only that, but -- you guessed it -- he's <i>also</i> an alien who's come to subjugate Earth!)\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Biggest Blunder</b>]\n\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3] -- Doc Ock calls Spider-Man \"Super-Man\"\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:TOS_44_iron0|TOS #44] -- the cover (and splash page, not to mention the rest of the entire magazine) calls this month's menace \"The Mad Pharoah!\" in what was probably the most blatant spelling error of all time (should be \"pharaoh\")!&nbsp; (Not to be outdone, however, was [LINK:1963:FF_19|FF #19|2026-12-04] calling Rama-Tut a \"pharoah\" in his own right!)\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08] -- Artist Jack Kirby can't decide how many toes the Hulk has, changing it from anywhere from 3 to 5!\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17] -- Stan and Jack completely lose track of time, leaving those poor X-Men in the Danger Room for a \"few days!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Boy, that Professor X is <i>tough</i>!)]\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01] -- Bruce Banner transforms, not only into the Hulk, but also to \"Bob\"\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:TOS_51_iron0|TOS #51|2027-04-16] -- somehow the Scarecrow swims from New York all the way to Cuba, easily illustrating how poor a geography teacher Stan Lee would make!\n\nWhile elapsed time and geography gaffes certainly have more effect on a story, one really can't get over the blatant cover spelling error, so this year's biggest blunder simply <i>has</i> to go to [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Tales of Suspense 44]!\n\nLong live [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"The Mad Pharoah!\"]\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Best Credit Box</b>]\n\nIt's probably not fair to grant an award based on a sample size of 1, but December's [LINK:1963:TOS_51_iron0|TOS #51|2027-04-16] was the first I'd seen of any \"playfulness\" in the credit box.&nbsp; <i>Hardly</i> worth more than a chuckle though, so I choose to hold off this award until things <i>really</i> get crazy!\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Biggest Surprise of the Year</b>]\n\nOne look at the incredibly bland cover of [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Amazing Spider-Man 10] ([LINK:1963:ASM_10|ASM #10|2027-04-05]) would never lead one to expect greatness, which is why I found myself surprised by its outstanding quality, even in a month that did not even feature one of Spider-Man's mainline villains.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Nothing about the cover suggests that it contains one of the most compelling mystery stories of the year.]\n\n[LINK:1963:ASM_5|ASM #5|2026-12-01] at least deserves an Honorable Mention, for who on <i>Earth</i> could have anticipated Spider-Man going up against <i>Dr. Doom</i>!\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Spiral Award</b>]\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:JIM_91_thor0|JIM #91] -- Sandu goes from \"Wow, look, I can levitate things!\" to \"I can rule the world!\" over the course of three pages, all while Loki pulls the strings from up in Asgard.&nbsp; (But <i>hey</i>, it's the Silver Age -- there's no time to <i>waste</i>!)\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:JIM_95_thor0|JIM #95] -- Professor Zaxton goes from \"world's foremost theoretical physicist\" to maniacal would-be ruler of the world in only 8 pages, creating an evil duplicate of Thor in the process, not to mention entire office buildings, planes, and ultimately himself -- logic be damned!&nbsp; I kept waiting for Mister Mxyzptlk to appear -- it seemed right up his alley.&nbsp; <i>Argh!</i>\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1|2026-11-16] -- Sub-Mariner's takeover of Manhattan is surprisingly quick, taking only three pages out of an annual that had plenty of pages to spare!\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:XMEN_3|XMEN #3|2027-03-10] -- The Blob learns that he is a mutant, escapes from the X-Men's mansion, takes over the carnival where he was employed, and attacks the X-Men's headquarters over the course of only <i>five pages</i> (9-14) -- the very <i>definition</i> of plot acceleration!\n\nChoosing a winner was difficult, but I'd say that the crazy Professor Zaxton (not to mention Stan's) doubling down on dumb from [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Journey Into Mystery 95] was the low point in terms of a plot spiraling out of control!\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Somewhere in New York City, there's still an <i>entire building</i> full of duplicated people who are now old enough to collect Social Security!)]\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Best and Worst Things to Happen to Marvel This Year</b>]\n\nThe [SPAN: severity=success; text=best thing] for this year was the further establishment of the shared Marvel Universe.&nbsp; The in-universe guest appearances that were only <i>hinted</i> at in late 1962 actually bore fruit this year, and we finally began to see some real crossovers (with Stan and his artists seeming to <i>really</i> focus on the Spider-Man/Human Torch relationship).\n\nBut I think it's fair to point out an Honorable Mention, which is the willingness near the end of the year to lean into the cliffhanger endings.&nbsp; This was shown mainly in later issues of <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> and <i>Tales to Astonish</i>.&nbsp; The sample size wasn't enough to push this across the finish line though.\n\nAs for the [SPAN: severity=error; text=worst thing], I have to go with the fact that Stan Lee remains about the only plotter on staff.&nbsp; I know he has help from his artists, who come up with the panel-to-panel flow, but as of now, he's still the primary storyteller.&nbsp; It's a major [SPAN: severity=error; text=single-point-of-failure] risk!\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Trope of the Year</b>]\n\nThere were two recurring finalists for the award this year.\n\nFirst off was the plethora of stories that involved someone attempting to steal secrets and sell them to the [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Communists,] (keep in mind that the \"Reds\" were <i>last </i>year's Trope).\n\nOne really has to ask:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Why would anyone want to sabotage their country in such a way?]\n\nJust to name a few (and this list is by no means comprehensive):\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17]\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09]\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:TOS_51_iron0|TOS #51|2027-04-16]\n\nBut ultimately, it was the recurrence of the dreaded [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=castle] that struck me as way overused, be they hidden, abandoned, or heavily-guarded!\n\nIt was simply <i>astonishing</i> how often castles played a role in the plots (again, not comprehensive):\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:ST_111_torch1|ST #111torch] (Human Torch)\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:ST_111_strange2|ST #111strange] (<i>and</i> in Doctor Strange)\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:JIM_100_thor0|JIM #100|2027-03-04]\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:TTA_52_giant0|TTA #52|2027-03-07]\n[HANG] * [LINK:1963:TOS_50_iron0|TOS #50|2027-03-24]\n\nI don't know if Stan secretly wanted to <i>own</i> a castle, but <b>boy</b> do they ever seem to be a prominent feature in these stories, [SPAN: severity=info; text=so I hereby award it the trophy for the year.]\n\nEven though those were the primary offenders, I also still think there are <i>way</i> too many stories involving \"I am your master.&nbsp; You are my slave.\" and alien invasions from other galaxies, all lining up to conquer Earth!&nbsp; (But those are holdovers from 1961 still resurfacing time and again...)\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>State of the Line Report</b>]\n\nBelow is the list of characters who have their own feature, sorted from best to worst in terms of average grade.\n\nSpider-Man (<b>7.53</b>)\n[BR] Fantastic Four (<b>4.60</b>)\n[BR] Iron Man (<b>3.70</b>)\n[BR] Ant/Giant-Man (<b>3.54</b>)\n[BR] Thor (<b>3.53</b>)\n[BR] Doctor Strange (<b>3.23</b>)\n[BR] Human Torch (<b>3.11</b>)\n\nYou'll note that Hulk, Avengers and X-Men are <i>not</i> listed for the simple reason that none of them had enough volume to make the list.&nbsp; (Hulk published only one issue in 1963, and the other two had only published three each).&nbsp; Including them would have been an unfair representation (though see my note on Doctor Strange below).\n\nWith a weighted average of <b>7.53</b>, [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Amazing Spider-Man] stands alone as Marvel's strongest title in 1963.\n\nThe nearest competitor was [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Fantastic Four], managing only a <b>4.60</b>, illustrating just how large the gap currently is between Spider-Man and the rest of the line.\n\nWhile <i>Fantastic Four</i> remains the line's most ambitious magazine and continues to introduce many of Marvel's most important concepts, it only just managed to average a readable score for the year.&nbsp; Despite all of its imagination and importance, the title never exceeded a 7.0 grade and still produced two [SPAN: severity=error; text=atrocious] issues along the way.\n\nNo other title is currently delivering quality with anything <i>approaching</i> Spider-Man's consistency.&nbsp; (In fact, <i>Journey Into Mystery </i>was the only one with an offering good enough to exceed mid-grade, [SPAN: severity=info; text=and it did this only <b>once</b>.])\n\nBeneath Spider-Man and the Fantastic Four lies a [SPAN: severity=warning; text=giant traffic jam of mediocrity], with Iron Man, Ant/Giant-Man, Thor, Doctor Strange, and the Human Torch, all separated by less than a single grade point.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It's a testament to how statistics help tell a different story than memory.)]\n\nIt's also important to remember that Spider-Man and the Fantastic Four are <b>full-length magazines</b>, and FF had an annual mixed in there.&nbsp; Below is the same list of characters, this time listed with their total weights rather than the actually weighted grades across all stories.\n\nSpider-Man (<b>9</b>)\n[BR] Fantastic Four (<b>14.33</b>)\n[BR] Iron Man (<b>7</b>)\n[BR] Ant/Giant-Man (<b>6.75</b>)\n[BR] Thor (<b>6</b>)\n[BR] Doctor Strange (<b>2.07</b>)\n[BR] Human Torch (<b>7</b>)\n\nSpider-Man started the year as a bi-monthly magazine and so did not get a weight of 12 (what one would expect for a monthly feature), whereas the Fantastic Four had an annual mixed in (with multiple stories to boot), so it got a total weight of <i>more</i> than 12.&nbsp; With the exception of Doctor Strange, most of the others were half or three-quarter-length stories, so they never added up to 12.\n\nAs for the Master of Mystic Arts, even though there were 7 graded stories, all were short, none of them ever exceeding a weight of 0.33.&nbsp; Three in fact, were even <i>shorter</i> at 5 pages apiece.&nbsp; One could argue that this small sample size results in misrepresentation, as his <b>3.23</b> does not carry the same weight as, say, the Human Torch's poor <b>3.11</b>.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=That is absolutely a fair point,] but since there were 7 offerings for this character, I still felt he deserved a place in the list.\n\nFor now, I wanted to begin showing some of the statistical trends behind the grades.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Future years will go into more detail and explain the breakdown more comprehensively,] but I felt it worthwhile to introduce the numbers as early as 1963.\n\nConsider this: [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"Title Analysis <b><i>Light</i></b>\"]\n\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=large; text=<b>Overall Impression</b>]\n\nThough the quality score of [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=4.48] is not as high as last year's <b>4.67</b>, it was almost certainly because of the vast increase in volume this year (about double).\n\n[HANG0] While a good quarter of the stories were at or above mid-grade, let's be honest:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=more than half of the output was <b>not</b>.]\n\nThere were three or four months in there where the outlook appeared very grim, and indeed, the May issues may well be the worst thing that Marvel has ever published to date.&nbsp; I can't imagine there being a worse month coming, but... the day is still young.\n\nOne is almost forced to conclude that other comic companies must have been publishing some truly [SPAN: severity=error; text=Dreadful Content] for <i>this</i> batch of Marvels to appear better.&nbsp; It was a rougher year than 1962, yet with a vast array of characters now to work with, there seems to be plenty of promise on the horizon.\n\nAs you can see in its very diplomatic responses in the letters pages, Marvel remains very respectful of its competition and even encourages its fans to <b><i>read</i></b> above all else.\n\nYou get the sense that Marvel is like a newborn calf -- struggling to get on its feet.\n[BR] (Or, to put it another way, Marvel is becoming more <i>interesting</i> faster than it is becoming <i>good</i>.)\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=It wasn't quite the <i>face-plant</i> I wondered about in my 1962 Year-End Summary, but the jury is definitely still out...]"
}